    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate focusing on high-ceiling speed profiles, headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (71.35) and **Brice Turang** (67.42). **Corbin Carroll** is our clear priority play; despite facing a tough arm in Nola, his projection benefits significantly from a projected #2 spot in the order, amplifying his potential to manufacture runs and steal bases. Similarly, **Brice Turang** earns a strong endorsement against Holmes. While Holmes\u2019s ability to hold runners typically dampens stolen base projections, the wind-out conditions and **Brice Turang**\u2019s leadoff positioning create a high-floor efficiency score that makes him a must-start for our 5x5 Roto chase. **Mickey Moniak** (62.71) also warrants attention; a massive 14% park boost combined with an elite SP skill matchup against Povich makes him a leverage play, even with a platoon disadvantage.\n\nThe bench decision process was tight, with **Cole Young** (40.21) serving as the final player cut. While **Cole Young** offers upside, his efficiency score fell just short of our utility threshold due to a difficult matchup against Tolle, which dragged down his base projection despite favorable park factors. We prioritized the stability of **Isaac Paredes** (53.10) in the UTIL slot to capture his platoon-neutral utility against Kay. \n\nRisk management remains paramount today, particularly with **Gabriel Moreno**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Corbin Carroll** carrying \"Lineup Pending\" status. While our model assumes their respective batting orders, any shifts downward in the lineup will necessitate an immediate re-evaluation of their efficiency scores. Furthermore, while we are starting **Seiya Suzuki** (52.75) due to his order-based advantages, the matchup against Harrison is an objective concern; his low SP skill score of -19% highlights a high-risk, high-reward profile that we are accepting to chase speed-heavy production. We are closely monitoring the weather and confirmed lineups to ensure our speed-focused strategy remains optimized through first pitch.",
